      <span class="category">Renters</span>
      <h1>How to Document Landlord Promises (So They Can't Deny Them Later)</h1>
      <time datetime="2026-03-23">March 23, 2026</time>
    </header>

    <p>Your landlord texts you: "I'll have someone out to fix the AC by next Friday." You screenshot it — because you know that text might matter later.</p>

    <p>But then what?</p>

    <p>That screenshot joins the 2,000+ others in your camera roll. When Friday comes and goes with no repair, you scroll through months of memes and receipts trying to find it. If you're lucky, you find it. If you're unlucky, you've already lost the dispute before it starts.</p>

    <h2>The Real Problem Isn't Taking Screenshots — It's Using Them</h2>

    <p>Renters are already documentation machines. Every text from a landlord about repairs, deposits, lease terms, or move-out conditions gets screenshotted. The instinct is right — these messages are informal contracts.</p>

    <p>The problem is that a screenshot sitting in your camera roll is passive. It doesn't:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Remind you when the promised deadline passes</li>
      <li>Compile into a timeline when things escalate</li>
      <li>Organize by landlord, property manager, or issue</li>
      <li>Generate a summary you can forward to a lawyer or housing authority</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What Actually Works</h2>

    <p><strong>1. Screenshot everything in writing.</strong> If your landlord says something over the phone, follow up with a text: "Just confirming — you said you'd fix the AC by Friday." Then screenshot that.</p>

    <p><strong>2. Track deadlines, not just promises.</strong> A promise without a deadline is just words. When your landlord says "next week" or "by the end of the month," note the actual date. If they miss it, that's documentation.</p>

    <p><strong>3. Build a timeline, not a pile.</strong> One screenshot proves one thing. A timeline of screenshots proves a pattern. "Three repair promises in two months, zero completed" is far more powerful than "they said they'd fix it."</p>

    <p><strong>4. Use a system, not your memory.</strong> Your camera roll isn't a system. You need something that extracts the commitment, tracks the deadline, and alerts you when it passes. That's exactly what Seddly does — it turns the screenshots you're already taking into an active accountability ledger.</p>

    <h2>When Things Escalate</h2>

    <p>If you end up filing a complaint with a housing authority, going to small claims court, or withholding rent (where legally permitted), a compiled timeline of documented promises is your strongest tool. It shows:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>You gave the landlord reasonable time</li>
      <li>They made specific commitments</li>
      <li>They repeatedly failed to follow through</li>
      <li>You documented everything in good faith</li>
    </ul>

    <p>This isn't about being adversarial. It's about making sure that when someone makes you a promise, you can hold them to it.</p>

